					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-305334#post-1084280</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-305334/crunch#post-1084280</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Fri, 25 Mar 2011 23:35:56 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Cherry Pict</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>395147</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>i have a few mild beefs with this</p> <ul> <li>humanizing 173 by giving it a motive, i think, ruins the mystique of the SCP. you're supposed to be afraid of it because no one, not even the guy who wrote the article, knows why it breaks necks. trying to make it sympathetic knocks it down several pegs from "unknowable horror" to "saturday morning cartoon villain". a sympathetic humanoid SCP can be shown as having a motive and it works, but 173 is a <em>statue</em></li> <li>i can't really take this piece seriously because 173's thoughts are expressed in the same fashion as, say, all of tarzan's lines from those old black and white film serials. the cultural damage has already been done - simplistic speech patterns like this are now only used by the incredible hulk and temporally displaced neanderthals in wacky sitcoms. this is a trope that has been beaten to death, yes, but it's still in the public mind and i sincerely doubt anything anyone does will change it anytime soon</li> <li>crunch sounds like a breakfast cereal for fat children</li> </ul> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
